Star catalog provenance
=======================

bright.bin
  Magnitude-sorted naked-eye catalog, 12,191 stars in the STR2 format: a
  J2000/ICRS direction, apparent magnitude, display color, and the catalog id
  the star came from (Gaia DR3 source_id, or Hipparcos number).

  Built offline by tools/starcat/build_bright.py from the snapshots committed
  under tools/starcat/data/ — ESA Gaia DR3 gaia_source (source_id, ra, dec,
  phot_g_mean_mag, bp_rp, parallax; phot_g_mean_mag < 6.5) and the Hipparcos
  New Reduction (hip, ra, dec, hp_mag, b_v, plx; hp_mag < 2.5), which supplies
  the bright end Gaia saturates on. The web application never contacts ESA or
  CDS at runtime.

  Display color is derived, not drawn: BP-RP (or B-V for the Hipparcos rows)
  to an effective temperature, then a blackbody spectrum through the CIE 1931
  observer to sRGB, quantized onto 24 levels. The relations are cited in
  tools/starcat/colour.py — Mucciarelli & Bellazzini 2020 (RNAAS 4, 52) for
  BP-RP, Ballesteros 2012 (EPL 97, 34008) for B-V, and Wyman, Sloan & Shirley
  2013 (JCGT 2(2)) for the color matching functions.

Named-star callouts
  The checked-in 50-entry catalog at public/stars/named.json is
  position-matched to bright.bin. Proper names follow the IAU Catalog of Star
  Names. Constellations, spectral classifications, and parallaxes were checked
  against SIMBAD and the Hipparcos New Reduction (CDS VizieR I/311). Distances
  are rounded for legibility; values for luminous supergiants whose parallax is
  poorly constrained are approximate rather than false-precision reciprocals.

Gaia LOD
  public/stars/lod is generated from ESA Gaia DR3 gaia_source fields source_id,
  ra, dec, phot_g_mean_mag, and bp_rp with phot_g_mean_mag <= 12. It is produced
  offline by tools/starcat/build_star_lod.py into 768 per-tile files plus
  g9.bin and a manifest. The web application never contacts ESA or CDS at
  runtime.

Star detail (sky_star_detail)
  Built offline by tools/starcat/build_detail.py and loaded into Postgres. It
  merges, per star, Gaia DR3 gaia_source and astrophysical_parameters, the HYG
  v3 database, the IAU Catalog of Star Names, the IAU constellation boundaries,
  and a SIMBAD lookup limited to the bright catalog. Each row records the
  catalogues it drew on.

  Where a star's constellation is given by both HYG and the boundary walk, the
  panel shows HYG's: the two disagree on 13% of the stars HYG names, and on
  every one checked (Rigil Kentaurus, Mimosa, Fomalhaut, Deneb Algedi) HYG is
  the one that agrees with the IAU boundaries. The computed value stands where
  HYG has no row, which is every star fainter than the HYG catalogue reaches.

